 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "Your street needs a sidewalk, so you pour one long slab of concrete.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "It looks perfect. For about a month.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Then cracks shoot across it in random places.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Concrete shrinks as it dries, and again on every cold night.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "One long slab can't move, so it tears itself apart.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So you cut grooves every few feet, called control joints.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "It still cracks, but now it cracks inside the grooves, where you can't see it.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Then you plant a tree next to it for shade.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Ten years later, its roots grow right under the slab and push it up.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "One edge sticks up a few centimeters, and people trip on it every day.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So you dig a plastic wall into the soil, a root barrier.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "It sends the roots downward, away from the concrete.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "But rain sits on the flat slab, and in winter it turns to ice.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So you tilt the slab, just two percent, toward the street.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now the water runs off before it can freeze.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Congratulations! You just built a sidewalk. What should we build next?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  }
 ],
